---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const drains = assertion('three-quarters-of-the-county-drains-badly')
const prime = assertion('prime-only-because-it-was-drained')
const clay = assertion('the-black-swamp-here-is-clay')
const two = assertion('two-files-a-hundred-and-thirty-times-apart')
const north = assertion('the-wet-ground-runs-north-and-west')
const seat = assertion('the-county-seat-is-on-the-driest-ground')

const spec = (a: typeof drains, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const drainsSpec = spec(drains, 'Per cent of the county')
const primeSpec = spec(prime, 'Per cent of the county')
const northSpec = spec(north, 'Per cent hydric soil')
const seatSpec = spec(seat, 'Per cent hydric soil')
---

<Article slug="prime-if-drained">
  <p>
    Every account of this county opens by calling its land flat and poorly drained, and then moves on
    to the canal or the railway or the oil. The soil survey turns that adjective into a number, and
    the number is larger than the adjective suggests. It is also the only source that describes the
    ground itself — not what has been built on it, grown out of it or taxed off it, but what is
    underneath all three.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={drains} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="drains"
    title="How Allen County drains"
    caption="Acres weighted by each soil component's share of its map unit, over 260,340 acres in 80 map units. Hydric soil is ground that formed under standing water."
    columns={['Measure', 'Share']}
    rows={drains.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="drains" set:html={JSON.stringify(drainsSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Two and a half acres in a hundred drain well. That single figure explains a great deal about the
    place: why it was settled late, why its ditches and tile are as extensive as they are, and why
    almost every field in it is a piece of engineering as well as a piece of ground.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={prime} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="prime"
    title="Prime farmland, and the condition attached"
    caption="The survey's own farmland classification. A further 8.7 per cent is not prime farmland under any condition."
    columns={['Class', 'Share']}
    rows={prime.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="prime" set:html={JSON.stringify(primeSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The commonest entry in the survey's farmland column is not a yes or a no. It is a conditional —
    <em>prime farmland if drained</em> — and it covers three quarters of the county. Of all the land
    here capable of being prime farmland, five acres in six qualify only with the water taken off.
  </p>

  <h2>Clay, not peat</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={clay} />
  <p>
    A swamp suggests peat, and peat soils cover fourteen acres of this county's two hundred and sixty
    thousand. What is actually here is clayey glacial till in three soil series — Blount, Pewamo and
    Glynwood — which between them are three quarters of the county, sitting at three positions on a
    very slight slope. Water stood on this ground not because the ground was made of what rotted in
    it, but because nothing beneath it would let water through.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the swamp was</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={two} />
  <p>
    The Great Black Swamp was drained between the 1850s and the 1890s, so no present-day survey of
    what grows here can find it. The soil can. A soil that formed under standing water stays hydric
    after the tile goes in, which is why the two files above are both correct and a hundred and
    thirty times apart.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    That is a rule, not a coincidence. A present measurement may answer a past question only where
    the property being measured survives the change in question. Soil, landform and section lines
    carry the past forward; land cover, land use, ownership and employment are overwritten by the
    very event you are asking about. And the claim keeps the proxy's tag: <em>35.3 per cent of the
    county is hydric</em> is established, <em>that is where the swamp was</em> is not, and the
    question stays open until a mapped historic extent turns up.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={north} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="north"
    title="Hydric soil by township"
    caption="Soil map units clipped to township boundaries. Marion, in the north-west, is the wettest of the twelve; Spencer sits in the far south-west and is nearly as wet."
    columns={['Group', 'Hydric']}
    rows={north.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="north" set:html={JSON.stringify(northSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={seat} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="seat"
    title="The driest ground in the county"
    caption="Lima against the county it sits in, and against Bath Township, which the city grew into."
    columns={['Place', 'Hydric']}
    rows={seat.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="seat" set:html={JSON.stringify(seatSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Which is the sort of thing a soil map is for. The county commissioners platted Lima in 1831,
    eleven years after the county was named and before most of it was settled, and the ground they
    chose is the driest in it — under a quarter as wet as Marion Township, and the only part of
    Allen County where two thirds of the land drains at least moderately well. Whether they knew
    that or simply found the high ground between two creeks is not a question a soil survey answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it does answer is the shape of what came after. Lima and the three townships wrapped around
    it hold seven of every ten people in the county, and all four sit in the drier half of it; the
    four wettest townships lie along the northern and western edges. Nobody chose the soil, and
    almost everything since has been arranged around it.
  </p>
</Article>
